--[[
	Several generations can be in flight at once, so the result of one is no
	longer a single card that the next run overwrites. Each generation owns a row
	here from the moment it starts until the user dismisses it: progress while it
	runs, then the finished animation with Install and Refine, or the error with
	a Try again.

	Newest first, because the thing you just asked for is the thing you are
	waiting on.
]]
-- The conversation open on the Chat tab: nil means a new one, and the first
-- generation adopts the lineage it creates.
local currentChatRoot = nil

--[[
	Has this chat started? Not "does it have a saved version yet" — started.

	The moment the first message is actually sent, this stops being a new chat,
	even though the server has not yet said what its id is and the tree is still
	empty. Without that, the seconds between clicking Generate and the job being
	accepted are a window where the composer still says NEW CHAT — and a second
	click in that window quietly starts a SECOND animation instead of editing the
	first.

	A new animation is therefore only ever started two ways: opening the plugin,
	and "+ New chat" in History.
]]
local chatStarted = false

--[[
	Which room the user is in, as a number that changes every time they move.

	A generation is a closure that runs for minutes. When it finally returns, the
	user may have started a new chat, opened a different one, or run two more
	generations. Every one of those closures used to write to the GLOBAL "current
	chat", so a late reply would quietly convert whatever room you were standing in
	into the one it came from.

	So each submission captures the epoch it was made in, and anything that touches
	shared chat state after an await first checks that the user has not moved. If
	they have, the job still finishes and still installs — it just stops trying to
	steer a room it no longer belongs to.
]]
local chatEpoch = 0

-- Messages mid-flight, counted PER ROOM. A single global counter locked every
-- conversation's composer for the length of one POST somewhere else; a boolean
-- was worse still, since two overlapping submissions let the first to return
-- unblock the composer the second owns.
local submitting = {}

--- The room a message belongs to. A chat with no id yet is identified by the
--- epoch it was opened in, so a brand-new chat is still a distinct room.
local function roomKey(root, epoch)
	return root or ("new:" .. tostring(epoch))
end
